// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27os/call_crypto_probe.t27 -- the host side of call_core.t27: the operating system's own crypto behind
// the call core's decisions, and a program that shows the behaviour. Not part of the app; nothing here decides.
// Crypto comes from macOS, never from this file:
//   * ephemeral P-256 keys and ECDH: Security.framework SecKeyCreateRandomKey (through keysync_host.ks_gen_pair)
//     and SecKeyCopyKeyExchangeResult with kSecKeyAlgorithmECDHKeyExchangeStandard (the raw shared x);
//   * SHA-256: CC_SHA256; HMAC-SHA256: CCHmac (CommonCrypto, libSystem); random bytes: SecRandomCopyBytes;
//   * AEAD: AES-128-GCM from CommonCrypto's CCCryptorGCMOneshotEncrypt / CCCryptorGCMOneshotDecrypt. These take 12
//     arguments, and t27b passes at most 8 (cli/t27b/README.md, "A function with more than 8 parameters"), so the
//     call goes through the system libffi (ffi_prep_cif + ffi_call, /usr/lib/libffi.dylib, -lffi), which puts
//     arguments 9-12 on the stack as AAPCS64 wants. The GCM fns are exported by libcommonCrypto but are SPI: the
//     public CommonCryptor.h of this SDK declares no GCM at all. CCCryptorCreateWithMode (public) also takes 12
//     arguments, so it would need the same route. The probe checks the GCM call against a published test vector
//     (McGrew-Viega test case 2) before it trusts it, and HMAC against RFC 5869 A.1.
// HKDF (RFC 5869) is two HMAC calls; the inputs are laid out by call_core.t27 and nothing else is computed here.
//
// Runs (print one line per check; exit 0 only when every check passed):
//   (no env)              vectors, ECDH agreement, then two endpoints in this process: a call with matching codes
//                         (session opens, frames accepted), a person who says "differ" (FAILED, frames refused),
//                         a changed answer nonce in transit (codes differ; the peer's CONFIRM does not open),
//                         a changed reveal (commitment fails), every byte of a frame flipped (AEAD refuses, the
//                         replay window does not move), the same frame twice (replay refused).
//   CK_ROLE=caller|callee two processes over UDP. CK_BIND / CK_PORT (default 127.0.0.1, caller 47741, callee
//                         47742), CK_PEER / CK_PEER_PORT (default 127.0.0.1 and the other port), CK_FRAMES (500),
//                         CK_PACE_US (2000), CK_SAY=match|differ (the person's verdict, given on the command line:
//                         the probe has no screen), CK_ANSWER=1 (the callee's person consents to answer; without it
//                         the call rings and ends unanswered). Each side prints CODE <6 digits>; the operator checks
//                         both are equal. Each side sends CK_FRAMES sealed 960-byte frames and its middle frame a
//                         second time (an injected replay), then BYE. Pass: every frame accepted once, 0 lost, the
//                         one replay refused, 0 AEAD failures.
// Test identities only: the fingerprints are fixed bytes, not keysync device keys (that binding is the next slice).
// Build (Apple silicon):
//   t27b build specs/t27os/call_crypto_probe.t27 -o ccp.o --macho
//   ld -arch arm64 -platform_version macos 11.0 $(xcrun --show-sdk-version) -syslibroot $(xcrun --show-sdk-path) \
//      -e _main -lSystem -lffi -framework CoreFoundation -framework Security -o ccp ccp.o
// Behaviour checks, not a security audit. Refs gHashTag/t27#11560.
module T27osCallCryptoProbe;

